Hook When it runs Typical use <code>on_settled</code> Once, after the flush that mounted the component has committed. May return a cleanup. Imperative DOM access, focus, third-party widgets. <code>on_cleanup</code> When the owning scope (component, effect run, hole, or row) is disposed. Cancel timers, detach listeners, close subscriptions. <code>on_hydration_end</code> Once, after <code>hydrate</code> has committed the page; on the next tick in a page that wasn't server-rendered. Work that must wait until hydration is over. <code>create_effect</code> First on the flush after mount, then on each tracked change, always after the DOM commit. Side effects driven by reactive state. <code>create_memo</code> At creation unless <code>lazy=True</code>; later reads bring changed inputs current. Derived values. <code>create_memo</code> with <code>async def</code> Starts at creation unless <code>lazy=True</code>; revalidates when tracked sources change. Data fetching with <code>Loading</code>. <pre><code>from wybthon import component, create_effect, create_signal, html, on_cleanup, on_settled


@component
def Pinger():
    count, set_count = create_signal(0)

    on_settled(lambda: print("mounted"))
    on_cleanup(lambda: print("unmounted"))

    create_effect(count, lambda n: print("count is", n))

    def ping():
        set_count(lambda n: n + 1)

    return html(t"&lt;button onclick={ping}&gt;ping&lt;/button&gt;")
</code></pre> <p>Order of events for one mount:</p> <ol> <li>The body runs once and returns the template instance; the effect and callbacks are registered but nothing has run yet.</li> <li>The template mounts (one clone command) and the flush commits the DOM.</li> <li>The effect phase runs the effect: <code>count is 0</code>.</li> <li>The settled queue runs: <code>mounted</code>.</li> <li>Each click stages a write; the handler returns, the graph flushes, and the effect prints the new count.</li> <li>On unmount the owner is disposed: the effect is torn down and <code>unmounted</code> prints.</li> </ol>

Created during Parent owner Disposed when Component body the component's scope the component unmounts A hole's expression or the subtree it mounted the hole's scope the hole re-evaluates or unmounts A <code>For</code> row the row's owner the row leaves the list <pre><code>from wybthon import Props, component, create_effect, create_signal, html


class TimerProps(Props):
    interval: int = 1000


@component
def Timer(props: TimerProps):
    count, set_count = create_signal(0)

    # Body effect: lives until the component unmounts.
    create_effect(count, lambda n: print("count is", n))

    # Binding: re-runs only when count changes.
    return html(t"&lt;p&gt;Elapsed: {count()}&lt;/p&gt;")
</code></pre> <p>A binding or hole whose first run reads nothing reactive, and creates no children, cleanups, or async work, can never run again. The renderer disposes it right away and keeps only the DOM it produced. A binding whose expression makes no calls goes further: <code>{props.label}</code> first runs under a lightweight probe, and when it reads nothing reactive (the parent passed a constant), the value is applied and no computation is allocated at all. So <code>{props.label}</code> over a constant prop costs no computation, and <code>{props.label.upper()}</code> costs nothing after its first run.</p>

You want Write The value to stay live in the DOM Place it in the markup: <code>&lt;p&gt;{props.name}&lt;/p&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;p&gt;Hi, {props.name}&lt;/p&gt;</code> To derive from it Read it inside a memo or effect: <code>create_memo(lambda: props.name.upper())</code> A one-time read (a seed, a config flag) <code>untrack(lambda: props.name)</code> <pre><code>from wybthon import Props, component, create_memo, create_signal, html, untrack


class ProfileProps(Props):
    name: str
    initial_tab: str = "info"


@component
def Profile(props: ProfileProps):
    tab, set_tab = create_signal(untrack(lambda: props.initial_tab))  # seed: read once
    shout = create_memo(lambda: props.name.upper())  # derive: tracked read
    return html(t"""
      &lt;div&gt;
        &lt;p&gt;{props.name}&lt;/p&gt;
        &lt;p&gt;{shout()}&lt;/p&gt;
        &lt;p&gt;Tab: {tab()}&lt;/p&gt;
      &lt;/div&gt;
    """)  # bind: place in the markup
</code></pre> <p>Passing a prop through to a child stays reactive without any care on your part: <code>&lt;{Avatar} name={props.name}&gt;</code> hands the child a getter, so the child reads the parent's current value.</p>

How do I declare a component's props? <p>Subclass <code>Props</code>, annotate its fields as you would a dataclass, and take one parameter annotated with it. Reading <code>props.label</code> returns the parent's value, tracked, as in Solid:</p> <pre><code>from collections.abc import Callable

from wybthon import Props, component, html


class SaveButtonProps(Props):
    label: str = "Save"
    on_save: Callable[[], None] | None = None


@component
def SaveButton(props: SaveButtonProps):
    return html(t"&lt;button onclick={props.on_save}&gt;{props.label}&lt;/button&gt;")
</code></pre> <p>In the template, the compiler turns <code>{props.label}</code> into a getter, so the text updates when the parent passes an expression that changes. A component with no inputs takes no parameters. See Authoring patterns.</p> How do I pass a callback to a component? <p>Annotate the field as a callable, such as <code>on_save: Callable[[], None] | None = None</code>. Reading <code>props.on_save</code> returns the function exactly as the parent passed it. Fields with other annotations unwrap accessors and zero-argument functions on read, so a callback annotated <code>Any</code> would be called by the read itself.</p> Why does my type checker reject <code>prop(0)</code>? <p>Most fields don't need <code>prop()</code>: write an ordinary default (<code>count: int = 0</code>). For a mutable value, use <code>prop(default_factory=list)</code>, passed by keyword; type checkers only recognize a field specifier's default when it's given by keyword.</p> How do I pass children? <p>Subclass <code>ParentProps</code> and place <code>props.children</code> in the template. Callers nest markup inside a component tag, <code>&lt;{Card} title="Hi"&gt;&lt;p&gt;Body&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/{Card}&gt;</code>, or pass it with item syntax, <code>Card(title="Hi")[html(t"&lt;p&gt;Body&lt;/p&gt;")]</code>, or the <code>children=</code> keyword.</p> Is the <code>&lt;{Card}&gt;</code> tag form type-checked? <p>Not statically. Type checkers can't see inside a template string, so dev mode checks a tag's props when it runs, like any component call. When you want the checker's help, call the component inside an interpolation instead: <code>{Card(title=user().name)}</code> is checked against <code>CardProps</code> like any other call, and the compiler still passes <code>title</code> as a getter, so it stays reactive.</p> Do I need a mypy plugin? <p>No. <code>Props</code> uses PEP 681 <code>dataclass_transform</code>, so pyright, Pylance, and mypy check component calls out of the box. See the typing guide.</p> Where did <code>Router</code>, <code>Link</code>, and <code>form_state</code> go? <p>The core lives in <code>wybthon</code>; the router, forms, virtual lists, and scheduling helpers live in their own modules. Import them from <code>wybthon.router</code>, <code>wybthon.forms</code>, <code>wybthon.virtual</code>, and <code>wybthon.scheduling</code>:</p> <pre><code>from wybthon.forms import bind_text, form_state
from wybthon.router import Link, Route, Router, navigate
</code></pre>
